**Classes & OOP in Python**
--> OOPs -- Object-Oriented Programming
--> based on the concept of objects which bundles data and functions together
--> helps to organise code into reusable and modular parts
KEY TERMS IN OOPs :-
| Concept                      | Description                               | Example                      |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| **Class**                    | A blueprint/template for creating objects | `class Car:`                 |
| **Object**                   | An instance of a class                    | `car1 = Car()`               |
| **Attribute**                | Variable inside a class                   | `self.color = "Red"`         |
| **Method**                   | Function inside a class                   | `def start(self):`           |
| **Constructor (`__init__`)** | Special method to initialize objects      | `def __init__(self, brand):` |
| **`self`**                   | Refers to the current object              | `self.brand = brand`         |
FOUR PILLARS OF OOPS are ;
1) Abstraction 
--> hiding the unnecessary details and showing only necessary details of data
2) Inheritance
--> ability of one class (child) to acquire the properties and behaviors of another class (parent)
3) Encapsulation
--> Hiding internal details; using methods to access data
4) Polymorphism
--> Same function name but different behaviors
